<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll">
  <p>The London Museum was preparing to launch a new website and wanted to make sure it actually worked for people with disabilities, before going live rather than after.</p>
  <p>I recruited and ran usability testing sessions with 11 participants across four disability groups: visual impairments, mobility difficulties, cognitive impairments and hearing loss. Each person used their own devices with their own assistive technology setups, including screen readers, screen magnifiers, speech recognition software and closed captions. Sessions ran both in person and remotely via Zoom.</p>
  <p>The testing surfaced issues that automated accessibility checks would never catch. Moving elements on the homepage caused genuine distress for participants with vestibular and cognitive conditions. Screen magnifier users were losing navigation controls off the edge of their view. Deaf participants couldn't find the accessibility information because years of bad experiences on other sites had taught them not to expect it to be there.</p>
  <p>One participant, when she finally found it, said it was the best accessibility information she'd seen on any website anywhere.</p>
  <p>That's what accessible research surfaces. Not just whether a screen reader can parse the page, but whether a real person with real needs can actually use it.</p>
